🚀 Core Topics & Implementation Highlights
1. Categorical Encoding & Binning
One-Hot Encoding: Converting categorical variables into binary indicator columns using pd.get_dummies().

Dummy Coding: Dropping reference categories to prevent multicollinearity.

Continuous Binning: Grouping continuous data into discrete interval bins using pd.cut().

2. Messy Data & Outliers
Missing Value Imputation: Handling null values strategically across numeric and categorical features.

Outlier Filtering: Identifying and removing statistical outliers using standard deviation thresholds and the Interquartile Range (IQR) method.

3. Unstructured Text Feature Engineering
Text Standardization: Lowercasing and removing non-letter characters using regular expressions (str.replace()).

Metadata Feature Extraction: Calculating character counts, word counts, and average word lengths.

Count Vectorization (CountVectorizer): Converting text corpora into token count matrices, filtering out sparse terms using min_df and max_df.

TF-IDF Vectorization (TfidfVectorizer): Normalizing word counts to reduce the weight of overly common terms and increase the prominence of rare, informative words.

N-gram Modeling: Capturing contextual phrasing by extracting bigrams and trigrams via ngram_range.

4. Model Validation & scikit-learn Workflow
Pipeline Standard: Implementing the standard scikit-learn modeling lifecycle—instantiating estimators, fitting on training data (.fit()), generating predictions (.predict()), and evaluating performance.

Train-Test Splitting: Preventing data leakage by fitting transformers (like TF-IDF or scalers) exclusively on training data and transforming unseen test sets independently.

Accuracy Metrics: Assessing model error using metrics such as Mean Absolute Error (MAE).

💻 Quick Reference Code Snippets
Text Vectorization & TF-IDF (Training vs. Unstable Unseen Data)
Python
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer

# Instantiate vectorizer
tv = TfidfVectorizer(max_features=100, stop_words='english', ngram_range=(1, 2))

# Fit and transform ONLY on training data
tv_transformed = tv.fit_transform(train_df['text_clean'])
train_tv_df = pd.DataFrame(tv_transformed.toarray(), columns=tv.get_feature_names_out()).add_prefix('TFIDF_')

# Transform test data using the fitted vectorizer
test_tv_transformed = tv.transform(test_df['text_clean'])
test_tv_df = pd.DataFrame(test_tv_transformed.toarray(), columns=tv.get_feature_names_out()).add_prefix('TFIDF_')
Basic Model Validation & Evaluation
Python
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor
from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error

# Instantiate model
model = RandomForestRegressor(random_state=42)

# Fit model
model.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Predict and evaluate
predictions = model.predict(X_test)
mae = mean_absolute_error(y_test, predictions)
print(f"Mean Absolute Error on Test Set: {mae:.4f}")
